=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
A thorough live inspection of the Kath3 lab found no fault affecting Location C (192.168.200.0/24). The reported "total isolation" does not exist in the network.

**End-host configuration:** PC11 (192.168.200.11/24), PC12 (192.168.200.12/24), PC13 (192.168.200.13/24), and PC14 (192.168.200.14/24) all have correct IP addresses and a valid default gateway of 192.168.200.1 (router4's eth0 on the sw4_r4 segment).

**Gateway and local segment:** Router4's eth0 is UP and assigned 192.168.200.1/24. Direct pings from all four PCs to the gateway returned 0% loss. Router4 can also ping back to all four hosts without issue.

**Routing plane:** RIP is running on router4 and correctly redistributes the 192.168.200.0/24 prefix to the rest of the network. Remote routers (router0, router3, and others) all carry valid RIP-learned routes to 192.168.200.0/24, and the reverse routes (to all remote subnets) are installed in router4's FIB.

**End-to-end connectivity:** PC11 successfully reached 10.16.1.1, 192.168.100.1, 10.50.1.1, 192.168.1.1, and 172.16.10.1 — spanning multiple remote sites — with 0% packet loss in every test.

**Root cause:** There is no fault. The user ticket describes a condition that is not present in the live lab; Location C is fully connected to the rest of the network and routing normally.

**Corrective action:** None required on the network side. The helpdesk should follow up with the reporting users to determine whether the symptom has already resolved on its own, whether they were testing incorrect addresses, or whether the ticket was filed in error.
=== END ===